  ],
  "text": "A normal bit is nought or one. A qubit holds a combination of both until you look at it. Which sounds like it checks every answer at once, and that is where most explanations go wrong. You cannot read a superposition. Measure it and you get one ordinary answer. The trick is not holding every answer. It is arranging the wrong ones to cancel out. Wrong paths destroy each other, and the right one survives to be measured. Which only works on problems with the right shape, and most problems do not have it. Factoring huge numbers has that shape, which is exactly what a lot of encryption rests on. Simulating molecules has it too, and that is the genuinely exciting one. But the machines need to be colder than deep space, and the error rates are still enormous. This will not replace your laptop. It will break some locks and help build some medicines. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
